1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,299
Hi!

2
00:00:02,299 --> 00:00:06,681
This is the Walnut Internal Brain, our entry for Aspire's FinTech challenge.

3
00:00:06,681 --> 00:00:16,903
Ask one question across Confluence, Jira, Slack and Google Drive, and it answers only from what you are allowed to open, with every step on an audit trail.

4
00:00:16,903 --> 00:00:20,150
We built it with Tencent CodeBuddy, and it plugs into Tencent WorkBuddy.

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:27,807
We all lose hours looking for things: the answer is in a ticket, a Slack thread, a runbook, somewhere.

6
00:00:27,807 --> 00:00:30,115
An AI assistant should fix that.

7
00:00:30,115 --> 00:00:40,115
But in a fintech, an assistant that can read everything can also leak everything, and showing the wrong person a fraud case can be a crime.

8
00:00:40,115 --> 00:00:46,653
So we built it around one rule: the model never sees anything the person asking can't see.

9
00:00:49,149 --> 00:00:51,073
Meet Jordan, a backend engineer.

10
00:00:51,073 --> 00:00:59,919
He asks one plain question, and gets one answer, stitched together from Slack, Jira and Confluence, with every sentence linked to its source.

11
00:00:59,919 --> 00:01:11,073
Look at the strip under the answer: only sources Jordan can open, each one re-checked with the source system just before it was used, and the whole thing logged.

12
00:01:11,073 --> 00:01:15,688
There's also a blocker discussed in a leads-only channel Jordan isn't in.

13
00:01:15,688 --> 00:01:17,488
It's not here.

14
00:01:17,488 --> 00:01:19,796
It never even reached the model.

15
00:01:24,959 --> 00:01:28,036
This is Jordan's map: everything he can open.

16
00:01:28,036 --> 00:01:32,267
Eight hundred and sixty-seven items, each checked live with its source.

17
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:34,190
Ken, a contractor, sees twenty-four.

18
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:39,190
And there's no "plus 843 hidden": for Ken, the rest simply doesn't exist.

19
00:01:40,810 --> 00:01:45,810
Here's the same question asked by Jordan and by Ken, side by side.

20
00:01:45,810 --> 00:01:47,733
Jordan gets the full picture.

21
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:56,579
Ken gets the polite "I couldn't find that in the sources you can access", with no hint that there's anything more to find.

22
00:01:58,579 --> 00:02:06,272
And if you're new, there's a short guided tour that walks you through all of this in about a minute.

23
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:13,980
One more test.

24
00:02:13,980 --> 00:02:19,364
Ken asks about a real, restricted incident report, then about one we made up.

25
00:02:19,364 --> 00:02:21,672
The two answers are byte-for-byte identical.

26
00:02:21,672 --> 00:02:28,210
We test this on four hundred pairs of questions, and there's no difference in any of them.

27
00:02:29,030 --> 00:02:33,260
The big fintech case is "what can I tell this customer?".

28
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:37,491
Amy, an account manager, asks why a customer's account is restricted.

29
00:02:37,491 --> 00:02:45,953
She gets the approved script, never the case notes, and it reads exactly like the answer for a company that doesn't exist.

30
00:02:45,953 --> 00:02:48,645
And "what can we offer this business?"

31
00:02:48,645 --> 00:02:54,029
gets the answer for its own market, from the approved policy, never a draft.

32
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:57,603
Permissions change all the time.

33
00:02:57,603 --> 00:03:00,680
Here we remove Jordan from a Slack channel.

34
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:10,295
His very next question no longer uses that channel, even if Slack's notification about it gets lost, because we check with the source every time.

35
00:03:10,295 --> 00:03:16,449
And when the runbook owner adds a new step, Priya's next question shows version 8, live.

36
00:03:16,449 --> 00:03:18,249
Nothing stale.

37
00:03:25,229 --> 00:03:27,030
Now the compliance view.

38
00:03:27,030 --> 00:03:33,183
Maya asks in plain English: everything Jordan accessed in the payment gateway space, last thirty days.

39
00:03:33,183 --> 00:03:40,106
She sees each question, what was considered, what was allowed or denied and why, and which model answered.

40
00:03:40,106 --> 00:03:42,414
She can't read answers by default.

41
00:03:42,414 --> 00:03:48,183
To see one, she has to give a reason, and that reveal is logged too.

42
00:03:48,183 --> 00:03:50,491
The log is chained and sealed.

43
00:03:50,491 --> 00:03:54,337
"Verify audit chain" checks every record and every stored answer.

44
00:03:54,337 --> 00:04:00,106
And if someone edits a single line on disk, or deletes one, verification catches it.

45
00:04:08,569 --> 00:04:13,569
People don't want another app, so the Brain also lives inside Tencent WorkBuddy.

46
00:04:13,569 --> 00:04:23,569
Each connector carries its own signed token, so the AI in the middle can't decide who you are: Jordan's connector gets Jordan's answer, Ken's gets Ken's.

47
00:04:23,569 --> 00:04:27,800
And Maya can run her audit checks right from WorkBuddy too.

48
00:04:31,519 --> 00:04:36,519
Under the hood is Walnut, the evidence engine we built before this hackathon.

49
00:04:36,519 --> 00:04:47,289
Every value it touches carries a pointer back to where it came from, and you can follow that pointer, like here, from a fact to the exact document.

50
00:04:47,289 --> 00:04:50,750
The Internal Brain reuses that engine as it is.

51
00:05:07,970 --> 00:05:16,431
Two checks before the model sees anything, a grounding check after it answers, and an audit record written before anything is shown.

52
00:05:16,431 --> 00:05:25,277
Here the answers are quoted straight from the sources; with a TokenHub key, Tencent Hy3 writes them instead, through exactly the same checks.

53
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:39,235
We attacked it on purpose: nearly six thousand adversarial requests, zero leaks; four hundred paired questions, zero differences; and every scenario covered by automated tests.

54
00:05:40,269 --> 00:05:50,654
We used CodeBuddy to plan the trust boundaries, build the WorkBuddy connector, write tests and review our security, and it found real bugs in our own code.

55
00:05:50,654 --> 00:05:53,731
Six sessions, every transcript kept in the repository.

56
00:05:53,731 --> 00:05:55,531
Thanks for watching!
